<section class="topic-kpi-section" aria-labelledby="kpi-topic-heading">
  <h2 id="kpi-topic-heading" class="kpi-topic-title">suggested KPIs for this topic</h2>

  <p class="kpi-topic-intro">
    These KPIs help leaders integrate play into work in ways that support productivity,
    innovation, mental health, and culture. They focus on understanding play types,
    giving permission, designing microvacations, shifting language, and running experiments.
  </p>

  <div class="expandable-container kpi-expandable-container">


    <!-- 1. Discovering & Using Preferred Play Types -->
    <div class="expandable-item">
      <h3 class="expandable-title" data-target="kpi-play-types">
        discovering &amp; using preferred play types
        <svg class="expand-arrow" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
             viewBox="0 0 331.65 100.99" style="width:24px">
        <polygon fill="#4e50a2"
                 points="0 0 161.57 100.99 331.65 0 0 0"/>
        </svg>
      </h3>

      <div class="expandable-content" id="kpi-play-types">
        <ul class="kpi-list">
          <li>Use the Identifying the Play Types in Your Team exercise to gather each member’s top 5 preferred play types.</li>
          <li>Identify at least 3 play types that appear most frequently across the team’s lists.</li>
          <li>Design team-building or lunch-hour activities that intentionally target those shared play types.</li>
          <li>Observe and record changes in energy, creativity, and team cohesion after play-integrated sessions.</li>
          <li>Review the team’s play-type map annually and update activities as team composition changes.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- 2. Permission, Playful Norms & Neutralizing Complaints -->
    <div class="expandable-item">
      <h3 class="expandable-title" data-target="kpi-permission">
        permission, playful norms &amp; neutralizing complaints
        <svg class="expand-arrow" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
             viewBox="0 0 331.65 100.99" style="width:24px">
        <polygon fill="#4e50a2"
                 points="0 0 161.57 100.99 331.65 0 0 0"/>
        </svg>
      </h3>

      <div class="expandable-content" id="kpi-permission">
        <ul class="kpi-list">
          <li>Explicitly give permission for play during breaks, occasional meetings, and low-stakes moments.</li>
          <li>Teach the team why play matters (restoration, creativity, connection, productivity) and how it supports work.</li>
          <li>Coach leaders not to shut down harmless fun unless it’s unsafe or genuinely harmful (e.g., discriminatory).</li>
          <li>Discourage “tattling” on play that meets agreed guidelines; address chronic complainers by resetting expectations.</li>
          <li>Model participation: leaders join playful moments occasionally to signal that play is valued, not suspect.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- 3. Microvacations & Work–Play Cycles -->
    <div class="expandable-item">
      <h3 class="expandable-title" data-target="kpi-microvacations">
        microvacations &amp; work–play cycles
        <svg class="expand-arrow" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
             viewBox="0 0 331.65 100.99" style="width:24px">
        <polygon fill="#4e50a2"
                 points="0 0 161.57 100.99 331.65 0 0 0"/>
        </svg>
      </h3>

      <div class="expandable-content" id="kpi-microvacations">
        <ul class="kpi-list">
          <li>Design short “microvacations” (5, 10, 15, or 30 minutes) that align with team play preferences.</li>
          <li>Ensure microvacations are accessible, low-cost, inclusive, and healthy (no reliance on junk food or alcohol).</li>
          <li>Incorporate at least one microvacation per week during intense project phases.</li>
          <li>Ask participants to briefly rate how they feel before and after microvacations to monitor impact.</li>
          <li>Refine microvacation options based on feedback and observed changes in energy and focus.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- 4. Language Shifts & Culture Accessories -->
    <div class="expandable-item">
      <h3 class="expandable-title" data-target="kpi-language-culture">
        language shifts &amp; culture accessories
        <svg class="expand-arrow" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
             viewBox="0 0 331.65 100.99" style="width:24px">
        <polygon fill="#4e50a2"
                 points="0 0 161.57 100.99 331.65 0 0 0"/>
        </svg>
      </h3>

      <div class="expandable-content" id="kpi-language-culture">
        <ul class="kpi-list">
          <li>Retire “work–life balance” and replace it with language about emotional wellbeing and full-life experience (including work).</li>
          <li>Replace “accomplishments” with “contributions” in performance conversations to reinforce shared purpose.</li>
          <li>Use culture accessories — mascots, mottos, rituals, symbols, and stories — to reinforce a playful, purposeful identity.</li>
          <li>Shift from “rewards and recognition” toward designing meaningful, shared experiences at work.</li>
          <li>Regularly assess: does our language make work feel like a dead zone between real life, or part of living fully?</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- 5. Experiments, Productivity & Play-Driven Innovation -->
    <div class="expandable-item">
      <h3 class="expandable-title" data-target="kpi-experiments">
        experiments, productivity &amp; play-driven innovation
        <svg class="expand-arrow" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
             viewBox="0 0 331.65 100.99" style="width:24px">
        <polygon fill="#4e50a2"
                 points="0 0 161.57 100.99 331.65 0 0 0"/>
        </svg>
      </h3>

      <div class="expandable-content" id="kpi-experiments">
        <ul class="kpi-list">
          <li>Run simple experiments using Productivity Through Play prompt sets (movement, laughter, creative outlets, connection, etc.).</li>
          <li>Track self-reported energy, focus, and productivity before and after play-based interventions.</li>
          <li>Encourage teams to test different play-infused practices and share the results on Twennie.</li>
          <li>Use The Game With No Name to identify helpful cultural practices and those worth adding.</li>
          <li>Summarize findings annually in an internal report, highlighting which practices improved productivity, engagement, and mental health.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>


  </div>

  <p class="kpi-topic-footer">
    Choose a KPI from each category to integrate play with work in a deliberate, strategic way —
    so your team can replenish energy, strengthen relationships, innovate more freely, and enjoy
    what they’re building together.
  </p>
</section>


<script>
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
    const titles = document.querySelectorAll('.kpi-expandable-container .expandable-title');

    titles.forEach(function (title) {
      title.addEventListener('click', function () {
        const parent = this.closest('.expandable-item');
        const targetId = this.getAttribute('data-target');
        const content = document.getElementById(targetId);
        if (!parent || !content) return;

        const isActive = parent.classList.contains('active');
        parent.classList.toggle('active', !isActive);
        content.style.display = !isActive ? 'block' : 'none';
      });
    });
  });
</script>
